# Reopen-blank fix — NEW architecture (server-owned buffer)

> The shipped design. Aligned with moving the terminal process to a separate server.

## Idea in one line
The **server** (where the PTY lives) saves the session's **real output buffer** to disk on hibernate and replays it on reopen. The **client just renders bytes** — it has no idea hibernation happened.

## Where the "last screen" lives
```
        ┌────────── CLIENT (renderer) ──────────┐
        │   xterm  ◄── renders bytes only        │   ← thin: no capture,
        │   (getBufferSince replay, same as a    │     no storage, no
        │    normal live-PTY reattach)           │     reconcile logic
        └───────────────────┬────────────────────┘
                            │ existing data channel
┌───────────────────────────┴───────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                MAIN  →  (standalone TERMINAL SERVER)                    │
│                                                                        │
│   PTY ─► RingBuffer ──persist on hibernate──►  disk  (terminal-buffers)│
│                       ◄─rehydrate on reopen──                          │
│   adapters know which providers reprint  ─►  inject reset into stream  │
└────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
        the session AND its history live together, on the server
```

## Flow
- **Hibernate:** server writes `RingBuffer → disk` (just before it frees memory + kills the process).
- **Reopen:** server rehydrates that buffer into the new session + spawns CLI `--resume`. The client's **existing** `getBufferSince` replay paints it instantly. For providers that reprint, the **server injects a terminal reset into the byte stream** right before the reprint, so the old screen is replaced cleanly — the client renders it without any special logic.

## Why this is the robust + sustainable one
- 🟢 **State with its owner.** History lives next to the PTY → survives reconnect, second windows, web/remote clients. Rides the main→server move unchanged.
- 🟢 **Source of truth, not a copy.** Persists the real output bytes; no lossy client re-render.
- 🟢 **One source of provider knowledge.** "Reprints on resume?" stays in the server adapters.
- 🟢 **Thinner client.** All new logic is server-side; the renderer reuses the replay path it already had. Main SQLite stays lean (buffers go to a flat file with a disk budget).

**Net:** the blank is eliminated at its root (the lost buffer is no longer lost), and every new responsibility lands on the correct side of the client/server boundary.
